s two general shapes in colours of their own and no grove gets one: round for the oak, ash and maple of Åland's wooded meadows, upright for the alder, aspen and rowan through its woods, with round more likely the more open a tree stands. A tree's height sets its stage of life, blended between young (branches to the ground), grown (a cleared trunk) and old (a long bare trunk under a small high crown; a pine's goes flat). Height is a tree's own, so a stage can't pop. A pine on rock is told by what the images say: short, with the canopy's mean over 32 m well under its own place's. That is what separates it from a young pine in a thicket, whose neighbours are as tall, and the aerial photo is about a quarter brighter under such trees than under short ones among others. Stand density doesn't help: it's near 1 on rock. It's about 13% of trees on the rocky L2433E and 2% on the mixed L2343C. A rock pine is thick and short, its crown a low, broad, flat spread, leans downwind of the south-westerly and its rings wander, each blended by how sure the signal is.
